WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:17.050
The Wright Brothers: First in Flight and Family

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.030
History is a delicate endeavor.

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:24.200
It tends to remember those who make a lasting
impact as larger-than-life figures, defined

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:30.759
by their contributions, as well as the the
effects the ripple and unfold after the fact.

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:35.320
It's easy to forget that many of the people
who shaped our world started out just like

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:36.320
everyone else.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.870
They had to obey their parents, for better
or worse.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:40.920
They played — and argued — with their
siblings.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.700
They got into trouble in school.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.399
They struggled to find a direction in life.

00:00:45.399 --> 00:00:48.149
The Wright brothers were two such ordinary
figures.

00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:52.710
They weren't born into wealth or power; they
didn't have clear goals in life for a long

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:57.300
time; they didn't even graduate from high
school, because of circumstances beyond their

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.300
control.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:03.970
But after their first flight on December 17,
1903, they set in motion a series of events

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:08.150
and advancements that had a profound effect
on the world.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:13.030
At first, no one believed the Wright Brothers
had developed an aircraft powered by an engine

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:19.000
and controlled by human drivers… yet just
38 years later, the Avro Lancaster — the

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.830
plane that would become one of the Allies'
most successful weapons during World War II

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.369
— rolled off the assembly line.

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:30.450
Before flight became a revolutionary development
in travel, diplomacy, logistics, or warfare,

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:36.229
it was the obsession of two brothers -- fueled
by curiosity, and powered by the influence,

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:39.979
love, and encouragement of an entire family.

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:45.820
A Pair of Happy Childhoods

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.190
It was Wilbur Wright who once said, "If I
were giving a young man advice as to how he

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:54.930
might succeed in life, I would say to him,
'Pick out a good father and mother, and begin

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.500
life in Ohio.'"

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:02.110
To truly understand Wilbur and Orville Wright,
we're going to need to start with their parents,

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:06.710
because in another environment, or in another
family, governed by other parents, it's entirely

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:11.370
possible these two gentlemen would have been
uninspired church janitors.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:16.720
The patriarch of the Wright family was Milton,
or as he was known to his brethren, Bishop

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:17.720
Wright.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:22.740
Born in 1828, he grew up working on his parents'
Indiana farm, where he practiced his public

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:27.740
speaking skills as he toiled away in the fields,
reading every book he could find, and developing

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.260
a firm belief in the evils of slavery.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:35.910
It was also in those fields that the 15-year-old
Milton had something of a religious epiphany,

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.600
one that he later explained like this:

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:44.030
"It was not by forces, visions, or signs,
but by an impression that spoke to the soul

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.020
powerfully and abidingly…

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:51.110
There was no sudden revolution from great
anguish to ecstatic joy, but a sweet peace

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:54.210
and joy [which I had] never known before."

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:59.840
It wasn't until 1847 that he joined the Church
of the United Brethren, rising quickly though

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.400
the hierarchy in an organization that recognized
his gift for giving sermons and relating to

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.620
people in an unprecedented way.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.670
He was eventually made a circuit preacher,
which means he traveled from church to church

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:12.990
across the state.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:18.140
One of the towns on his circuit was Hartsville,
where he not only visited the church, but

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.940
also saw a woman he had courted in college.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.460
They had separated after he developed a serious
illness, but he had never forgotten her.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:30.220
When the church reassigned Milton to the wild
frontier and gold mining towns of Oregon,

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:32.170
he asked her to marry him.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:36.690
She agreed that she would wait for him to
return, and if they still felt the same, they

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:37.690
would marry.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:41.350
Two years later, he returned to Indiana and
married Susan Koerner.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:46.990
Their first and second sons, Reuchlin and
Lorin, came along in 1861 and 1862.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:52.210
It was another five years before Wilbur was
born and, after the birth of twins who died

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:55.520
in their infancy, Orville was welcomed into
the family in 1871.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.410
Three years later, the family was completed
with the arrival of a sister, Katharine.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:04.080
For their entire lives, the siblings — particularly
Wilbur, Orville, and Katharine — remained

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.080
incredibly close.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:08.850
It was Katharine who once described their
childhood in terms that would make anyone

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.010
envious, saying,

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.820
"No family ever had a happier childhood than
ours.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:21.260
I was always in a hurry to get home after
I had been away half a day."

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.259
Changing the course of the world… one child
at a time

00:04:23.259 --> 00:04:27.180
The daily life of the Wright siblings was
strict and disciplined, but it was also a

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:32.090
warm, loving environment with parents who
encouraged their children's individual talents.

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:36.889
In some ways, Milton was staunchly conservative,
but he was also very progressive in his views

00:04:36.889 --> 00:04:40.610
on things like the abolition of slavery and
other social issues.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:45.169
He did meet his wife while she was studying
for a literature degree from Hartsville College,

00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:49.050
and in the mid-19th century, that wasn't exactly
normal for women.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:53.370
Milton continued to travel with his church
job and was away from the family for long

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:57.830
periods, but he made sure to stay active in
their lives; he sent them regular letters,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:02.449
gave them complete access to his extensive
library, and brought them gifts… including

00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:07.370
one that sparked an interest in Wilbur and
Orville that wouldn't truly develop for many

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:08.370
years.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:13.299
They called it "the bat," and it was a toy
helicopter that Milton gave the boys in 1878.

00:05:13.299 --> 00:05:17.949
It was based on the design of a French aeronautical
pioneer named Alphonse Penaud, and it was

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:22.729
essentially a rubber band attached to a propeller
that would send the toy shooting up into the

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:23.729
sky.

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:28.010
Wilbur and Orville — then 11 and 7 years
old, respectively — attempted to make their

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:32.330
own, larger versions of the toy, and while
they never quite pulled it off successfully,

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:35.259
it did kickstart a fascination with machinery.

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:37.090
They never forgot this gift, either.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:42.060
Decades later, they would write about how
that one simple toy, that one small gift from

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.750
a loving father, eventually guided the course
of their entire adult lives.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.849
Their mother, too, had a huge impact on their
love of all things mechanical.

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:55.289
Susan Wright had grown up working alongside
her father in his carriage shop, where she,

00:05:55.289 --> 00:05:58.449
too, nurtured a lifelong love of machines
and gadgets.

00:05:58.449 --> 00:06:03.340
Wilbur and Orville grew up watching her design
and build small appliances for use around

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.699
the house; they also played with toys she
had built for them.

00:06:05.699 --> 00:06:09.879
As adults, the brothers recognized the influences
their parents had.

00:06:09.879 --> 00:06:10.879
Orville once said,

00:06:10.879 --> 00:06:15.180
"We were lucky enough to grow up in an environment
where there was always much encouragement

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:20.770
to children to pursue intellectual interests;
to investigate whatever aroused curiosity.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:25.860
In a different kind of environment, our curiosity
might have been nipped long before it could

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.219
have borne fruit."

00:06:27.219 --> 00:06:29.259
That's not to say everything was easy.

00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:33.439
Because Milton's position in the church involved
so much travel, the whole family often found

00:06:33.439 --> 00:06:38.090
itself uprooted as well, moving alongside
him as he established training schools for

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:40.710
those interested in learning the church's
theology.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:44.689
At one point, Milton even helped found a seminary
college.

00:06:44.689 --> 00:06:49.560
But all was not well within the church, and
in the years after Katharine's birth, a schism

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:54.710
developed between those who wanted the church
to stay the same as it had always been, and

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:59.099
the more liberal-minded folks who were looking
to change the church’s stance on everything

00:06:59.099 --> 00:07:04.340
from social issues to secret societies.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.949
Milton was on the side of the conservatives,
but he was in the minority.

00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:12.949
By 1881, he had been removed from his position
as bishop and returned to the same Indiana

00:07:12.949 --> 00:07:16.379
circuits he had preached on when he first
courted his wife.

00:07:16.379 --> 00:07:21.900
Yet even as a church drama played out on a
national scale with him directly in the center,

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:28.009
something far more dramatic was happening
in the Wright household: Susan was diagnosed

00:07:28.009 --> 00:07:29.470
with tuberculosis.

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:33.680
By this time, the precocious boys who had
been captivated by a flying toy were in high

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:34.680
school.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:38.879
Orville was gravitating toward science and
technology -- he was known for being equal

00:07:38.879 --> 00:07:44.189
parts clever and mischievous, yet when he
was around those he didn't know, he was almost

00:07:44.189 --> 00:07:45.599
painfully shy.

00:07:45.599 --> 00:07:49.569
Like many brilliant students, he had trouble
applying himself to a curriculum, so instead

00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:55.139
of going through his required, junior-year
classes, he opted for college prep courses.

00:07:55.139 --> 00:07:59.379
Unfortunately that disqualified him from getting
a high school degree, so he decided he simply

00:07:59.379 --> 00:08:01.490
wasn't going to bother with school anymore.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.099
Wilbur didn't graduate high school, either.

00:08:03.099 --> 00:08:05.189
The family moved again before he could finish.

00:08:05.189 --> 00:08:09.409
He eventually skipped the end of high school
and went straight into college prep classes,

00:08:09.409 --> 00:08:14.490
with the hope of going to Yale and becoming
a teacher, but fate had other things in mind.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:20.129
Where Orville was the shy one who loved practical
jokes, Wilbur was confident, eloquent, and

00:08:20.129 --> 00:08:23.759
always level-headed… until the winter of
1885.

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:28.490
That unfortunate season saw Wilbur playing
ice hockey with a group of friends, when he

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:30.770
was hit in the face by a rogue stick.

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:34.470
He lost most of his upper teeth and was housebound
for weeks.

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:39.190
The untimely accident would only get worse
-- complications from the injury turned into

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:44.290
digestive issues, heart palpitations, and
long bouts of depression that made him abandon

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:45.350
his hopes of Yale.

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:49.950
Instead, he spent the next few years of his
life caring for his already-ill mother, until

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:51.710
her death in 1889.

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:57.330
Now, history is a strange thing, interconnected
in ways that are nothing short of bizarre…

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.230
so here's where it's worth taking an aside.

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:05.680
The boy who hit Wilbur was named Oliver Crook
Haugh, and while it's unclear whether it was

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.630
an accident or on purpose, we can say that
he was known as a neighborhood bully.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:14.250
Haugh — who was working at a local drug
store — later became addicted to one of

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:18.830
the most typical medicines of the era: Cocaine
Toothache Drops.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:23.840
The combination of drugs, alcohol, and erratic
behavior saw him committed to an asylum, and

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:28.120
when he got out, he ended up reading Robert
Louis Stevenson's The Strange Case of Dr.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:29.230
Jekyll and Mr. Hyde.

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:33.910
At first, he identified with Jekyll, believing
his newly earned medical degree was going

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:35.530
to help him in finding a great cure-all.

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:41.320
But as his cocaine habit continued, and he
wrote that he was becoming more like Mr. Hyde.

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:46.160
Oliver’s true descent into darkness started
six years after that 1885 incident on the

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:53.010
ice; between 1891 and 1905, it's believed
he killed at least sixteen people — starting

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:57.240
with his fiance's father, and including his
own mother, father, and brother.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.850
In 1907 — the same time Wilbur and Orville
were trying to get people to take their inventions

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:05.810
seriously — he was executed for his crimes.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:10.770
Finding purpose in each other

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:16.290
The passing of a beloved parent spawns a grief
well beyond words, and everyone deals with

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.270
that grief in their own way.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:21.310
Within days of burying Susan, Milton was back
at work.

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:25.100
Wilbur and Orville attacked work as well,
embarking on a journey to start a publishing

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:26.650
business and newspaper.

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:34.960
Wilbur was to serve as the editor, and Orville
as the publisher.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:39.600
It was called the West Side News, and it wasn't
as much of a familial departure as you might

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:44.070
expect -- their father had been the editor
of a publication called the Religious Telescope,

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:47.820
and while they were still in school, they
had printed their own newsletter called The

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:48.820
Midget.

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:51.770
While it had honorable beginnings — the
idea was that they were going to advertise

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:56.000
a fledgling printing business — their parents
quickly put them out of business when they

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.700
found out they intended to run a column called
"The Inherent Wickedness of Schoolchildren"

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:03.120
with a byline attributed to one of their teachers.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:06.810
So after the death of their mother, Orville
and Wilbur set about building a new printing

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:07.810
press.

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:11.970
Well, it was "new" to them, anyway -- it was
made from a broken tombstone, parts from an

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:15.120
old buggy, and other miscellaneous bits-and-bobs.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:18.960
The brothers were immediately successful,
thanks in part to their prior knowledge of

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:24.000
the industry, but also because they had an
immediate injection of regular business.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:28.650
Buoyed by a partnership with the Church of
the United Brethren, they expanded their printing

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:33.400
house, built a second press, and accepted
at least one contract to build a similar press

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:34.740
for another business.

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:39.710
With commerce booming, the young entrepreneurs
began billing themselves as the "Wright Brothers."

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:44.600
This was about the same time a new transportation
craze was sweeping across the US.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:48.720
It was called "wheeling," and it came about
with the invention of what was then called

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:50.030
the safety bicycle.

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:54.280
It's basically the bicycles we know today,
with two wheels of similar size.

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:59.370
Wilbur and Orville were both passionate cyclists,
regularly going on long trips and even winning

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:00.370
cycling races.

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:04.980
Their mechanical aptitude allowed them to
do what many people can only dream of: turn

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:07.130
their passion into their livelihood.

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:11.320
After friends and fellow cyclists started
asking them for help in repairing their bikes,

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.600
the Wright brothers realised they could build
something better.

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:15.700
So they did.

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:20.020
In addition to their old printing press and
their new repair business, they started building

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:24.580
two models of bikes, each named after early
Ohio pioneers.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:29.460
The Van Cleve was their high-end model, named
for their great aunt, Mary Van Cleve, who

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:33.350
had been the first person to set foot on the
land that would become Dayton, Ohio.

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:36.740
They also had a St. Clair model that was more
accessible.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:41.340
The Wright Brothers were again successful,
but it was just one more stepping stone in

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:42.350
their life's pursuits.

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:47.860
That next step is the logical continuation
of an adventurous biker’s mindset.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:13:30.410
Hopping on your bike, heading off on a grand
trip, cresting a hill and speeding downwards…

00:13:30.410 --> 00:14:23.840
well, it's

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:26.360
a little bit like flying, isn't it?

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:36.060
Aerodromes and dirigibles and ornithopters…
oh, my!

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:40.940
The Wright Brothers were far from the first
people to fly, to look down and see the ground

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:45.680
passing far beneath their feet in what had
to be an experience both exhilarating and

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:46.680
terrifying.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:50.010
Even as they were busy designing and building
their own bicycles, the headlines were captured

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:53.261
by news from the burgeoning world of aeronautics.

00:14:53.261 --> 00:14:58.250
At the time, it was all highly experimental,
and there were a lot of failures, but none

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:03.080
captured the Wright Brothers' imagination
like that of Otto Lilienthal.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:07.540
Lilenthal was a German aeronautical engineer
responsible for developing groundbreaking

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:12.020
theories, like the idea that a curved wing
shape would provide more lift than a flat

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:13.020
wing.

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:17.000
He wasn't just a theorist -- he designed 16
different versions of gliders and took them

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:20.440
on almost 2,000 flights… albeit, brief flights.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:24.580
It was cutting-edge work for the time, but
the limitations of the gliders became tragically

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:27.490
clear on August 9, 1896.

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:32.160
Lilienthal was flying in one of his monoplane
gliders when it was caught by a sudden gust

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:33.160
of wind.

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:37.570
The nose was pushed upwards, his forward momentum
was stalled, and he died the following day

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:40.330
after breaking his spine in the ensuing crash.

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:45.200
As newspapers reported on the death of the
aviation pioneer, Wilbur sat vigil beside

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:46.200
his brother.

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:51.380
Orville had contracted typhoid fever — bedridden
for several months, it wasn't until October

00:15:51.380 --> 00:15:55.600
that he was lucid enough to understand what
his brother was telling him about Lilenthal's

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:58.440
death and the engineering mysteries behind
it.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:05.690
The crash illustrated just how vital it was
to maintain control over a flight path, limiting

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:09.590
the impact of external factors like rogue
wind gusts.

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:13.380
While Wilbur had nursed his brother through
his potentially deadly illness, he had returned

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:15.860
to that forgotten interest they'd had as children.

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:19.810
Wilbur headed to the library to find books
on the principles of flying, and when Orville

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:24.570
was once again coherent, they agreed: it was
a lack of control that had led to Lilenthal's

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:25.570
death.

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:28.080
That was something they might be able to work
on.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.710
As it turned out, the timing was fortuitous.

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:35.420
The popularity of bicycles meant more and
more companies were beginning to mass produce

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:39.990
them, and smaller businesses like the Wrights'
were being boxed out of the market.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:44.730
Even though Orville assured his father they
were doing fine and business was booming,

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:46.280
they saw the writing on the wall.

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:50.270
So when word got out that the US government
was throwing obscene amounts of money at the

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:55.630
research of manned flight, it became clear
which project deserved their attention.

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:59.510
It was Wilbur who came up with the idea of
adjusting the wings of a craft to capture

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:04.050
wind and guide it, leading them to design
a kite with controls that allowed them to

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:05.050
twist the wings.

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:09.630
It worked exactly like they'd planned, and
Wilbur — who hadn't been able to wait until

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:13.640
Orville returned from a camping trip to try
their kite — hopped on his bike, headed

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:17.759
out into the woods, and told his brother they'd
cracked it.

00:17:17.759 --> 00:17:23.240
Off to Kitty Hawk

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:28.319
Anyone who has been to Ohio knows that it's
pretty flat, making it an inelegant location

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:30.419
to test experimental aircraft.

00:17:30.419 --> 00:17:35.159
The Wrights needed another plan, so they reached
out to the US Weather Bureau and asked for

00:17:35.159 --> 00:17:40.840
test locations that could give them elevation,
wind, and preferably a soft place to land.

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:45.799
Among the suggestions was Kitty Hawk, North
Carolina: a village that had a decent average

00:17:45.799 --> 00:17:49.929
wind speed, high dunes to launch from, and
— because the brothers had no illusions

00:17:49.929 --> 00:17:54.070
about what they were attempting — sand that
would offer a much more forgiving landing

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:55.820
than most other surfaces.

00:17:55.820 --> 00:17:59.999
So they headed off to Kitty Hawk, and yes,
their family was understandably worried — so

00:17:59.999 --> 00:18:04.570
worried that their father made them promise
to never, ever, both get into the plane at

00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:05.610
the same time.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:10.140
A crash in which he lost one son would have
been tragic; losing both sons would have been

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:11.180
too much to bear.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:15.669
The brothers ultimately kept their promise,
only taking one single flight together in

00:18:15.669 --> 00:18:16.669
1910.

00:18:16.669 --> 00:18:22.039
Their first biplane lifted off in 1900, but
this attempt was more of a kite than a manned

00:18:22.039 --> 00:18:23.039
glider.

00:18:23.039 --> 00:18:27.749
Flying it unmanned allowed them to make observations
and adjustments, and in 1901, the new and

00:18:27.749 --> 00:18:29.529
improved model was ready to go.

00:18:29.529 --> 00:18:34.340
Four miles outside of Kitty Hawk, the brothers
flew between 50 and 100 glides, but still

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:38.269
weren't happy with the progress they'd made
in the area of control.

00:18:38.269 --> 00:18:42.200
Realising they needed more information, the
brothers constructed their own wind tunnel

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:46.330
to test various shapes, sizes, and wing configurations.

00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:50.659
Their third glider really was spectacular,
and they flew hundreds of manned flights,

00:18:50.659 --> 00:18:52.820
built a control system, and added a rudder.

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:56.091
Finally, it was time to throttle up on the
power.

00:18:56.091 --> 00:18:59.340
The Wright Brothers recruited a machinist
who had worked with them on their bicycles,

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:01.240
a man by the name of Charles Taylor.

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:06.289
Together, they built a combustion engine and
propellor system, and it was this first powered,

00:19:06.289 --> 00:19:09.980
manned, fully-controlled flight that changed
the world.

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:14.429
The first flights lasted less than a minute,
but by 1905, they had perfected not only their

00:19:14.429 --> 00:19:19.470
technology but their own skills to the point
where they could stay in the air for a maximum

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:21.029
of 39 minutes.

00:19:21.029 --> 00:19:25.370
It wasn't long before they realised that solving
those first mysteries of flight wouldn’t

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:27.600
even be the most difficult challenge they
would face.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:31.320
The brothers had trouble convincing people
to believe their flights were even possible.

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:35.850
The brother set off on a series of demonstrations,
securing funding from the US Army and a group

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:38.500
of French investors for continued experiments.

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:44.500
In 1908, the US Army offered them $25,000
for a plane that could fly at least 40 miles

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:47.670
per hour, and carry a passenger as well as
a pilot.

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:49.110
They got to work.

00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:53.710
The Wright Brothers' success came fast, and
in the following years, Wilbur flew demonstrations

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:56.090
for audiences across Europe and America.

00:19:56.090 --> 00:20:00.990
By 1909, they incorporated the Wright Company,
and the Wright Exhibition Company came a year

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:01.990
later.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:05.259
Innovation and hands-on development soon gave
way to other matters, ones that had become

00:20:05.259 --> 00:20:09.610
more pressing: Wilbur was soon more occupied
with protecting their intellectual property

00:20:09.610 --> 00:20:15.090
and the rights to their invention, involving
the company in scores of lawsuits; much later,

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:20.039
they would be accused of delaying the advancement
of the very science they worked so hard on.

00:20:20.039 --> 00:20:24.399
Only three years after the establishment of
the Wright Company — on May 30, 1912 — Wilbur

00:20:24.399 --> 00:20:25.519
suddenly passed away.

00:20:25.519 --> 00:20:30.160
He had contracted typhoid fever, and was exhausted
by the toll the company had taken on him.

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:34.429
Orville only remained with the firm they had
established for another three years; he sold

00:20:34.429 --> 00:20:36.429
his portion of the company to investors.

00:20:36.429 --> 00:20:42.039
Not long after — on April 3, 1917 — Milton
Wright died in his home, peacefully, at 88

00:20:42.039 --> 00:20:43.039
years old.

00:20:43.039 --> 00:20:46.480
Orville continued to remain active in the
field, consulting with the government throughout

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:51.399
the world wars, and serving with organizations
like the National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics

00:20:51.399 --> 00:20:55.440
and the Daniel and Florence Guggenheim Fund
for the Promotion of Aeronautics.

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:59.999
It wasn't the preferred life of the brother
that had once been known as the shy practical

00:20:59.999 --> 00:21:03.920
joker, and for much of his remaining years,
he chose to spend the majority of his time

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:07.980
with friends and family in Ohio, or in his
Ontario vacation home.

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:13.070
Orville also embarked on a long-standing feud
with the Smithsonian, who had publicly claimed

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:17.500
in 1914 that it wasn't the Wright Brothers
after all who had conducted the first manned

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.730
flight, but Samuel Langley.

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:23.929
Determined not to allow their achievements
to be overshadowed, Orville fought not just

00:21:23.929 --> 00:21:29.110
for the retraction that came in 1928, but
for an apology — one that wasn't given until

00:21:29.110 --> 00:21:30.660
1942.

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:35.159
Six years later — on January 27, 1948 — Orville
had a heart attack.

00:21:35.159 --> 00:21:38.049
He died three days later, on January 30.

00:21:38.049 --> 00:21:41.970
By then, he had seen incredible things: their
invention, beginning with their first experiments

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:46.149
in controlling a kite over their sleepy hometown,
had taken on a life of its own.

00:21:46.149 --> 00:21:51.289
By his final decade, the bombers of World
War Two were changing history yet again.

00:21:51.289 --> 00:21:55.769
For all the impact Susan Wright had on her
children, she never lived to see them change

00:21:55.769 --> 00:21:57.830
the world, or even fly.

00:21:57.830 --> 00:22:03.279
Milton did, and he, like his children, eventually
took to the skies; he was 82 years old when

00:22:03.279 --> 00:22:07.590
he got into a plane, for the first and only
time, with Orville behind the controls.

00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:13.200
As they took off, he shouted from the passenger
seat: "Higher, Orville, higher!"

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:26.230
And they went higher.

